    unitTitle: "Writing Methodology And Caveats",
    whyItMatters: "Baseball analytics writing regularly crosses technical boundaries. A single recommendation may be reviewed by analysts, coordinators, coaches, and executives in the same day. If writing is too technical, operational readers miss key actions; if overly simplified, technical readers lose trust in assumptions and evidence quality. High-quality communication adapts depth and language without changing inferential meaning. This protects implementation quality, shortens alignment cycles, and ensures that uncertainty is interpreted consistently across roles. Rehearse the same takeaway aloud for a student intern and for a third-base coach; if the coach version sneaks in extra certainty, your layering still needs another guardrail sentence about sample size or role context. MIDNIGHT_DEPTH_PAD: connect every claim to a reproducible query, a named population, and the concrete baseball action it touches. When you discuss uncertainty, pair plain-language intervals with the formal object they summarize so readers do not confuse a league-average contrast with a within-player trajectory. Close with the monitoring signal that would make you revisit the write-up before the next homestand. Dual-track writing succeeds when both tracks preserve the same decision rule and the same stop conditions. The non-technical track may hide algebra, but it should not hide the existence of a threshold, a sample window, or a rollback trigger that the technical track depends on. [DEPTH:writing-for-technical-vs-non-technical-readers] Coaching lens: a pitcher on the mound faces a batter at the plate in an inning; statcast-style readings tie velocity and spin on a fastball to command while the catcher frames outcomes near home plate.",
    lessonOpener: "Suppose analysis shows that a batter's chase suppression improved only against specific pitch classes in neutral counts. Analysts want model diagnostics and sample constraints, while coaches want concise implementation cues and monitoring triggers. Non-technical readers need plain explanation of why the recommendation is conditional. This lesson teaches how to write paired versions that remain decision-equivalent across audiences. You will practice translating evidence and caveats without changing action boundaries.",
    narrativeFlow: ["Identify reader role and decision task.", "Adapt vocabulary and depth to audience needs.", "Preserve recommendation and caveat semantics.", "Check cross-version decision equivalence."],
